  "response": "Attributing a bridge collapse to design or to construction and maintenance is a matter of setting the physical remains against the paper record. Forensic engineering treats the two accounts as rival explanations of the same wreckage: a design deficiency should leave distress concentrated where the calculations were thin, while poor workmanship or neglect should leave it where the building and the upkeep failed. The viaduct collapsed during the morning peak traffic period. A magnitude 6.2 earthquake was recorded beneath the river minutes before the collapse; the ground motion came first, and the span came down only in the minutes that followed.\n\nForensic engineers mapped the debris field before any wreckage was removed, and that mapping led to the recovery of fractured gusset plate sections from the collapsed span. Metallurgical testing compared the steel's measured yield strength with the specified grade. Such comparison of measured properties with specified ones distinguishes steel that never met its grade from steel loaded beyond anything it was meant to carry. Investigators compared the as-built drawings against the original design drawings. That comparison shows that the installed gusset plates were thinner than the design specified. The design underestimated the dead load added by a later deck resurfacing, and at least one of these two deficiencies \u2014 the undersized plates or the unaccounted resurfacing weight, and perhaps both at once \u2014 must account for the capacity lost at the failed connection. Dead load is permanent, so an error in estimating it bears on the connection continuously from the day the overlay is laid.\n\nMaintenance records are read as traces of condition rather than as testimony, since a rating is only as good as the observations beneath it. Every inspection report filed in the decade before the collapse rated the structure in excellent condition, and that uniformly clean verdict cannot be squared with the deterioration logged at the bearings in the very same file. Corrosion at the bearings was noted in routine inspection records two years before the collapse, and that entry shows that deicing salt accelerated corrosion of the deck reinforcement.\n\nThat salt-accelerated attack on the deck reinforcement led the design firm to attribute the collapse to inadequate maintenance by the highway authority, whereas the construction contractor attributed the collapse to an under-strength connection detail in the design. Neither party's account can be adjudicated from the exhibits it chooses to present, which is why an official inquiry, with powers to compel the drawings, the mill certificates and the inspection file together, is the body that weighs the contributing causes. Although the design firm placed responsibility squarely on the authority's upkeep, the final report attributed the collapse primarily to an error in the original design. That ranking, rather than any accommodation reached between the disputing parties, is what fixes liability and drives the retrofit of comparable spans elsewhere.",
